==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*speḱ-}}
From {{uder|en|la|speciēs||appearance; quality}}, from {{m|la|speciō||see}} + {{m|la|-iēs}} suffix signifying [[abstract]] noun. {{doublet|en|spice}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈspiːʃiːz/|/ˈspiːsiːz/}}. Some speakers pronounce the singular with {{IPAchar|/-ɪz/}}, the plural with {{IPAchar|/-iːz/}}.
* {{audio|en|en-us-species.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːʃiːz|iːsiːz|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|species|pl2qual=rare, nonstandard|+}}

# [[type|Type]] or [[kind]]. {{q|Compare {{m|en|race}}.}}
#: {{ux|en|the male '''species'''}}
#: {{ux|en|a new '''species''' of war}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1871|author=w:Richard Holt Hutton|title=Essays, Theological and Literary
|passage=What is called spiritualism should, I think, be called a mental '''species''' of materialism.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=122|passage=He went on kissing her with unflagging industry, while she remained limply in his arms, in a '''species''' of satisfied trance.}}
## A group of plants or animals in which any two individuals of the appropriate sexes or mating types can produce [[fertile]] [[offspring]], typically by [[sexual reproduction]], usually having [[similar]] [[appearance]].
##: {{ux|en|This '''species''' of animal is unique to the area.}}
##* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=103|volume=II|text=Louise felt raised above her '''species'''; a voice had spoken within her inmost soul, whose revealings were vouchsafed but to the chosen few; and what had been indifference, was now disdain.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-01|author=Donald Worster| volume=100| issue=1| page=70| magazine=w:American Scientist| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/a-drier-and-hotter-future A Drier and Hotter Future]| passage=We may see many such [dust] storms in the decades ahead, along with '''species''' extinctions, radical disturbance of ecosystems, and intensified social conflict over land and water.}}
##*{{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title=w:Mass Effect 3|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2012|system=PC|scene=War Assets: Khalisah Bint Sinan al-Jilani|oclc=962368035|text=Westerlund News Reporter Khalisah Bint Sinan al-Jilani reached out recently to her viewers with a wartime plea for unity and cooperation among all galactic '''species'''.<br>UPDATED<br>Her sincerity touched extranet viewers and donations for war relief efforts are pouring in, both to the Alliance and its alien allies.}}
## {{lb|en|biology|taxonomy}} A [[category]] in the classification of [[organism]]s, ranking below [[genus]]; a [[taxon]] at that rank.
##* {{RQ:Darwin Origin of Species|text=Hence, in determining whether a form should be ranked as a '''species''' or a variety, the opinion of naturalists having sound judgment and wide experience seems the only guide to follow.}}
##* {{RQ:Schuster Hepaticae|volume=V|page=vii|text=Firstly, I continue to base most '''species''' treatments on personally collected material, rather than on herbarium plants.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-van-tassel David Van Tassel]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-dehaan Lee DeHaan]| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/wild-plants-to-the-rescue Wild Plants to the Rescue]| volume=101| issue=3| magazine=w:American Scientist| passage=Plant breeding is always a numbers game.{{...}}The wild '''species''' we use are rich in genetic variation, and individual plants are highly heterozygous and do not breed true. In addition, we are looking for rare alleles, so the more plants we try, the better.}}
## {{lb|en|chemistry|physics}} A particular type of [[atom]], [[molecule]], [[ion]] or other [[particle]].
## {{lb|en|mineralogy}} A [[mineral]] with a unique chemical formula whose [[crystals]] belong to a unique crystallographic system.
# An [[image]], an [[appearance]], a [[spectacle]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[image]] of something cast on a surface, or [[reflected]] from a surface, or [[refracted]] through a [[lens]] or [[telescope]]; a [[reflection]].
##: {{ux|en|I cast the '''species''' of the Sun onto a sheet of paper through a telescope.}}
## Visible or perceptible presentation; appearance; something perceived.
##* {{RQ:Dryden Annus Mirabilis|text=Wit,{{...}}the faculty of imagination in the writer, which searches over all the memory for the '''species''' or ideas of those things which it designs to represent.}}
##* {{RQ:Newton Opticks|text=the '''species''' of the letters illuminated with indigo and violet}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} Either of the two elements of the [[Eucharist]] after they have been [[consecrated]].
# Coin, or coined silver, gold, or other metal, used as a circulating medium; [[specie]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1727|author=w:John Arbuthnot|title=Tables of Ancient Coins, Weights and Measures
|passage=There was, in the splendour of the Roman empire, a less quantity of current '''species''' in Europe than there is now.}}
# A component part of [[compound]] [[medicine]]; a [[simple]].

====Usage notes====
* '''''Species''''' is singular and plural (like {{m|en|sheep}}, for example). {{m|en|specie|Specie}} is a separate word that means coin money.
* When '''''species''''' cooccurs with the possessive morpheme ''[[-']]'', it is generally still pronounced {{IPAchar|/ˈspiːʃiːz/}} ~ {{IPAchar|/ˈspiːsiːz/}}, not *{{IPAchar|/ˈspiːʃiːzɪz/}} ~ {{IPAchar|/ˈspiːsiːzɪz/}}.
* {{sense|biology|taxonomy}} See {{m|en|species name}}, {{m|en|binomial nomenclature}}.

